+
+11 rules, 12 positions
+
+<!-- END GENERATED sync-breakers -->
+
+The twelfth position is the second skipped character of the rule of state M that reads `_ri`; it is part of the same rule. The rules for `_o`, `_n`, `_r`, `_j`, `^o` keep the next character as text ("an unknown `_o?`: the o is dropped, the next character kept"); the others jump over it.
+
+**What happens.** The string with the smallest length that breaks the law through each rule is in the last column. On every one of them the C parser writes what the grammar writes (U6), so the table is a statement about the code and not only about the model. Three consequences are real in Hindawi's pipelines, which double every underscore of the translated program before they call the parser (twelve of the thirteen branches of `std2hin.awk`):
+
+- An identifier that has the letter `c` before an underscore loses the underscore. `tc_flag` reaches the parser as `tc__flag`; the filter writes `BD E8 B8 E8 66 D1 B5 E8`, which has no `5F`; the rule for `c` has swallowed the first underscore of the pair, and the second has become a marker in front of `f`. The escaped underscore is gone (U6).
+- An identifier with `v` after one of the consonants that begin a stem asks for a row that does not exist (`tvra`): `tv_set` crashes the filter (section 11.3; U6).
+- A rule that steps over the terminator reads whatever the buffer held before: `kakakaka _n` after another line gives different bytes depending on what came before (section 11.4).
+
+### 14.7 Twelve guards (a proposal, verified on the grammar, not applied to the C code)
+
+The repair adds twelve rules, each in front of one of the eleven rules above, each of the form *if the character that the rule would jump over unseen is `_` or the end of the input, do what the grammar would have done if the rule were not there*. The block is read by `tests/spec_model.py` (`load_repaired`), which puts each guard in front of its anchor rule. The last column is the shortest string that breaks the pairing law if that guard alone is left out.
+
+```rmn2acii-guards
+# state | guard rule (put in front of the anchor rule) | anchor rule | shortest string that breaks the pairing law without this guard
+L1  | 'c' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'c' -> skip 2 ; goto L2b 'ch'                   | c__
+L2b | 'v' [_ $]    -> emit stem 'a' ; goto L2a       | 'v' -> emit stem 'vra' ; skip 2 ; goto L2a     | lv__
+L2c | 'e' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'e' -> emit '_ee' ; skip 2 ; goto L1           | _e__
+L2c | 'y' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'y' -> emit '_yna' ; skip 2 ; goto L2a         | _y__
+L2c | 'o' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'o' . -> raw d ; skip 2 ; goto L1              | _o_
+L2c | 'n' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'n' . -> raw d ; skip 2 ; goto L1              | _n_
+L2c | 'r' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'r' . -> raw d ; skip 2 ; goto L1              | _r_
+L2c | 'j' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'j' . -> raw d ; skip 2 ; goto L1              | _j_
+M   | 'e' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'e' -> emit '^ee' ; skip 2 ; goto L1          | je__
+M   | 'o' [_ $]    -> raw c ; skip 1 ; goto L1       | 'o' . -> raw d ; skip 2 ; goto L1              | jo_
+M   | '_' [_ $]    -> goto L1                        | '_' -> emit '^_ri' ; skip 3 ; goto L1         | ^__a
+M   | '_' . [_ $]  -> goto L1                        | '_' -> emit '^_ri' ; skip 3 ; goto L1         | ^_^__
+```
+
+The last two guards test the two characters that the old rule for `_ri` jumps over; the explicit `^` that led to state M is dropped, as the rule `.` of M already drops it for other characters, and the underscore is read again in L1. A guard that sends the underscore to L1 never keeps it as text: a literal underscore comes only from a pair.
+
+**What the guards establish** (all of them are checked, U7):
+
+1. No rule of the repaired grammar can consume an underscore, or step over the terminator, unseen (the same static analysis that finds the eleven rules finds none).
+2. Law 3 holds without exception: for every string that the parser reads, in the ranges of the next paragraph, the output has one `5F` for every pair of underscores.
+3. The parser never reads past the end of the input. Together with a complete table this makes it a **total function**: every rule consumes at least one character or moves to a state that does, every state ends in a catch-all rule, no rule steps over the terminator, and every row that a rule asks for exists, so the parse of any string ends with an output. The first three conditions hold for the grammar with the guards; the last holds if the fifteen rows of section 11.3 are filled in.
+4. Each guard is needed: with the rule left out, the string in the last column of the block breaks the law, and with it the law holds.
+5. No guard fires on what the decoder writes for well-formed ACII: the parse of any two table keys written one after the other is unchanged, except where the shipped parser reads past the end (the key `^` followed by an underscore key: a halant at the start of a stretch, which is not well-formed ACII), and the round trips of section 14.5 are exact with and without the guards.
+6. The guards change the output only of strings that contain a letter that needs a partner (`c`, `_e`, `_o` ...) directly before an underscore or the end of the input, where the shipped parser jumps over the underscore.
+
+The ranges of the option `--full`: all 551,881 strings of at most four letters over `a-z` and `_`, and all 1,118,481 strings of at most five letters over `acehjklnortvyz_^` (the letters that the rules test): no violation, no read past the end. The option `--deep` extends the second range to the 17.9 million strings of at most six letters. The table counts the strings of at most three letters over `a-z` and `_` (20,440) for the shipped grammar and the repaired grammar, with the table as it is and with the fifteen missing rows filled in with a dummy byte:
