+
+**To apply them to the C code.** Each guard is a test in front of an existing branch: in `level1` for the letter `c`; in `level2b` for `v`; in `level2c` for `e`, `y`, `o`, `n`, `r`, `j`; in `matra` for `e`, `o` and for the `_` that follows an explicit `^`. A guard's action is the one the function already has for "any other character", so no new function and no new state is needed. This is not done in the package: the guards change what a handful of invalid inputs mean (`c_` is the letter `c` followed by a marker, not the letter CHA with its `h` missing), and the author of the project should decide that. A C patch would be checked against the repaired grammar by `tests/diff_parser.py`, which compares the grammar with the C parser on more than a million strings.
+
+### 14.8 Case folding, and arbitrary changes to the text (Q2)
+
+**What the code has.** The parser tests only lower-case letters, `_`, `^` and `|` in its guards; a capital letter is not a key letter and is passed through as text, with one exception, the wildcards, which keep any character. The lexers pass `[A-Za-z_]` runs to the parser, so capitals do arrive. The only place that folds is `Hindawi/hindrv/std2hin.awk`, which runs `tr 'A-Z' 'a-z'` on the program text for two of its language styles (`praa_tha`, `praa_thamika`), before the translation to Romenagri and before the escape `E` of section 14.5.
+
+**The algebra.** Let `phi` be that fold, letter by letter.
+
+- `phi` is a monoid homomorphism of strings, `phi(uv) = phi(u) phi(v)`, it is idempotent, it fixes `_`, digits and punctuation, and it is not injective (`A` and `a` go to `a`). It keeps every run of underscores: the number and lengths of the runs are the same before and after (U8).
+- Capital letters are inert in the parser: if every capital of `s` is replaced by `Q`, the output is the same up to that replacement (U8). So the restriction to lower case removes only letters that the parser would pass through anyway; it does not change the transducer on the strings that remain, and the laws of section 14.2 hold on both sides of the restriction.
+- The parser is not a homomorphism, with or without the restriction (`P(uv) != P(u) P(v)` in general: the halant, `vocalic`). It is closed under composition with `phi`: `P` after `phi` is again a sequential function.
+- `phi` does not commute with `P`, and it can create a breaker. The escaped text `C__f` parses to `C 5F f`. Its fold `c__f` parses to `B8 E8 66`: the letter `c` now swallows the first underscore (section 14.6) and the escaped underscore is lost (U8). With the guards, both keep the underscore.
+
+**Crash-resilience.** "Arbitrary string mutations by the host" include a case change, truncation at any point, insertion or deletion of any character, doubling or halving of underscores, and concatenation. For the grammar with the guards and a complete table, the parser is a total function (section 14.7, item 3), so none of them can make it fail: the claim is proved by the induction on the rules that item 3 states, and checked on all strings in the ranges of section 14.7. The checks include fifteen thousand random mutations of valid spellings in the default run, with the parser defined on every one (U8).
+
+For the shipped C code it is false, for four reasons:
+
+1. fifteen rows that the grammar asks for are missing (section 11.3), so a mutation that produces `kv`, `tv` and the other consonants before `v`, or `lh`, or `_rrh`, crashes the parser;
+2. eleven rules read an underscore or the terminator unseen (section 14.6), so a truncation after `c` or `_n` reads past the end of the input;
+3. the buffers have fixed sizes (section 11.4), so a long enough run overflows them;
+4. the decoder pops an empty stack on a stray halant or nukta (section 11.4).
+
+On those mutations (14,762 in the default run), the shipped grammar reads past the end of the input on 2.4% and asks for a missing row on 0.3%, and the shipped C parser crashes on 43 (U8). So the answer to "can it be formally verified as crash-resilient" is: *not as shipped*, and *yes, by the argument above, for the grammar of section 14.7 once the table is complete*. The proof is an induction and a finite exhaustive check, not a machine-checked proof; the connection between the grammar and the C code is the agreement test of `tests/diff_parser.py`, not a proof either.
+
+### 14.9 Joiners and operators (Q3)
+
+**Where the two alphabets meet.** Through the lexers, the parser sees only runs of `[A-Za-z_]`; the rest of the text, host operators and escapes included, is copied by the lexer (section 11.1). The parser changes 17 of the 95 printable ASCII bytes when each is given alone: the sixteen letters `bcdgjklmnprstwxy` and `_`. `acii2cf` and `acii2pcf` change none of the 95; `acii2rmn` wraps all 52 letters and `_` in `"e_x"` and leaves the other 31 punctuation marks, among them every operator of C, C++, Java and Python. So the script and the host languages share *one* character, the underscore, and the only context that has to be kept is the one of section 14.4: two states (U9).
+
+**The joiners that the converters cannot carry.** ZWJ (U+200D) and ZWNJ (U+200C) have no row in any table. `uni2acii` writes their low bytes, `0D` and `0C`, a carriage return and a form feed (section 11.6). ISCII encodes the same two things as the byte pairs halant-halant (explicit halant) and halant-nukta (soft halant); `acii2rmn` does not read them: `B3 E8 E8` comes out empty (the consonant is lost), `B3 E8 E9` crashes it. By accident the parser *writes* the first pair: `k_` is `B3 E8 E8`, because a `_` at the end is a halant and the consonant has one already. So the spelling exists in one direction and cannot be read back.
+
+**What a joiner costs.** The grammar is open to joiners at one place. A joiner key with an underscore in front is a family letter. Adding `_zwj` and `_zwn` takes two rules in state L2c and two rows in `acii.h`, and nothing else in the grammar (U9): the states, the longest guard and the list of rules of section 14.6 are unchanged, the marker rule holds, and the run law holds with them (`k___zwj` is KA, halant, a literal underscore and the joiner; `k__zwj` the same with the ordinary reading of `zwj`). The cost is in the other direction: the decoder has to know that a halant follows a halant, which is a look at the byte before, as it already does for the attribute prefix `EF`; and a byte has to be found (section 10.3), because the ISCII pairs are already the encoding of the halant.
+
+**The collisions that remain.** The projection of `acii2cf` is not injective: the Devanagari letter KA and the Latin word `ka` both come out as `ka` (U9). That is a design choice of the C-friendly filters, not an artefact of the underscore, and it is where the script and the host language cannot be told apart again after the fact. The underscore at the junction of a Latin word and Devanagari text (`trace_` followed by KA) is written `trace__ka` by `acii2cf` and `trace_ka` by `acii2pcf` (section 11.1): the first lets the decoder double it, the second does not.
+
+**The answer.** The minimal state-transition complexity to tell the underscore's roles apart is two states; joiners that obey the marker rule cost zero; the separation of script from host language costs no state in the parser because it is made, without memory, in the lexers.
+
